::STAT_U01_L07_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L07_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which display best shows the distribution of daily reading minutes for one group of students?
{
=A. Histogram#Correct. A histogram is appropriate for one numerical variable when the goal is to show distribution.
~B. Two-way table#A two-way table is for two categorical variables.
~C. Pie chart#A pie chart shows categorical parts of a whole.
~D. Segmented bar graph#A segmented bar graph compares categorical proportions.
}

::STAT_U01_L07_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L07_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which display best shows the relationship between hours studied and test score?
{
~A. Bar chart#A bar chart compares category counts.
=B. Scatterplot#Correct. A scatterplot shows the relationship between two numerical variables.
~C. Frequency table#A one-way frequency table does not show a numerical relationship.
~D. Pie chart#A pie chart shows categorical parts of a whole.
}

::STAT_U01_L07_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L07_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A bar chart for transportation method has one bar taller than the others. What does the tallest bar represent?
{
~A. The category that caused the other categories#A bar chart does not prove causation.
~B. The average of the category names#Category names cannot be averaged.
=C. The category with the greatest frequency#Correct. In a bar chart, taller bars represent larger counts or frequencies.
~D. A paired numerical relationship#Bar charts summarize categories, not numerical pairs.
}

::STAT_U01_L07_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L07_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
A segmented bar has segments of 20%, 30%, and 50%. What should the segments total?
{
~A. 50%#50% is only one segment.
~B. 30%#30% is only one segment.
~C. The number of categories#Percent bars total 100%, not the category count.
=D. 100%#Correct. A complete segmented bar represents the whole group.
}

::STAT_U01_L07_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L07_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
Why should a display choice be justified in context?
{
=A. It shows how the data type, variables, and question support the display#Correct. Justification connects the display to the statistical purpose.
~B. It proves the conclusion is causal#Justification does not prove causation.
~C. It lets the reader ignore labels#Labels are still necessary.
~D. It makes every display acceptable#Some displays do not fit the data structure.
}

